<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1509–<a href="/years/1579">1579</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada was a Spanish lawyer and conquistador who in 1536 led an expedition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-marta">Santa Marta</a> up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdalena-river">Magdalena River</a> into the highlands of the Muisca. He defeated the zipa of Bacatá and the zaque of Hunza, plundered the Sun Temple at Sogamoso in Iraca, and in 1538 founded Santafé de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> as capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. He later led a fruitless search for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-dorado">El Dorado</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gonzalo-jimenez-de-quesada.jpg" alt="Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ricardo Gómez Campuzano · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Licenciado_Gonzalo_Jim%C3%A9nez_de_Quesada.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada was an Andalusian lawyer, trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. His father, Luis Jiménez de Quesada, was a hidalgo, and he had two distant cousins among the conquistadors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a>. In 1535 he was appointed chief magistrate and second in command of an expedition to present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> led by Pedro Fernández de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucus-augusti">Lugo</a>, who had bought the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-marta">Santa Marta</a>. Quesada was then out of favour at home, having lost an important court case. The fleet carried over a thousand men, but Santa Marta proved a settlement of hovels and diseased colonists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1536 Fernández de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucus-augusti">Lugo</a> chose Quesada to lead an expedition into the interior. A land party went south from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-marta">Santa Marta</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdalena-river">Magdalena River</a> while a fleet carrying about 900 men sailed up it. From La Tora the party climbed the Opón River into the cordillera. Only 180 of 800 men survived the journey, reduced to eating snakes, lizards and the leather of their harnesses.</p><p>The Muisca had two rulers, one at Muyquytá and one at Hunza. Taking advantage of a war between them, Quesada's force subdued Muyquytá and then attacked Hunza successfully. Quesada took possession of the land in the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. At the end of 1538 Sebastián de Belalcázar arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolaus-federmann">Nikolaus Federmann</a> from Venezuela, and all three claimed the territory. Quesada persuaded them to take the dispute to the crown, and in July 1539 they sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartagena-colombia">Cartagena</a>. None obtained the governorship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Quesada returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> in 1550 and lived there for nearly thirty years as its most influential settler. He was governor of Cartagena from 1556 to 1557. In 1569, aged 63, he was commissioned to conquer the Llanos east of the cordillera. The expedition lost most of its livestock to a grass fire, reached the confluence of the Guaviare and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco</a> in December 1571 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> in December 1572 with 25 Spaniards. Suffering from leprosy and owing more than 60 thousand ducats, he died aged 70 in Mariquita, and his remains were moved to Bogotá in 1597. He wrote the Memoria de los descubridores (1567).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1537 fewer than 180 survivors of <b>Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</b>'s expedition, with horses and steel, overcame much larger Muisca forces, seized the zaque at Hunza and drove the zipa Tisquesusa into the hills, where he died of a wound.</blockquote><a href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca Confederation · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Spanish conquistadors led by <b>Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</b> subjected Bacatá, the main settlement of a Muisca confederation and seat of the zipa, ruler of the Bogotá savanna.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-20">20 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Spanish troops under <b>Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</b> reached Sugamuxi in 1537, and the Temple of the Sun burned down while soldiers looted it.</blockquote><a href="/state/iraca">Iraca</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</b> led an expedition up the Magdalena river and conquered the Muisca between 1537 and 1539.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-kingdom-of-granada">New Kingdom of Granada</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1537 <b>Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</b> seized Hunza and captured the aged zaque Quemuenchatocha.</blockquote><a href="/state/zacazgo-of-hunza">Zacazgo of Hunza</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</b> entered the savanna in 1537.</blockquote><a href="/state/zipazgo-of-bacata">Zipazgo of Bacatá</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Mariquita, in what is now <a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.18,-3.6&amp;year=1554" aria-label="Granada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4755 4384h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4755 4384h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.18,-3.6&amp;year=1554">What was here in 1554? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
